From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Simon Elsbrock <simon@iodev.org>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 11:45:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] gpio: amzncpld: hand child interrupts to their handlers

The port marked the CPLD's GPIO interrupts as nested threads, but the driver
dispatches them with generic_handle_irq() from its work function, as Amazon's
5.4 driver does with its non-nested gpiochip_irqchip_add(). A nested child
gets irq_nested_primary_handler as its primary handler, so every dispatch
warned "Primary handler called for nested irq", the consumer's thread never
ran, and the unacknowledged interrupt fired again about every 20 ms (light
sensor on irq 170, Echo Dot (5th gen) C2N6L4, 2026-11-26). Register the
children as ordinary interrupts, as the 5.4 driver did.

---
--- a/drivers/gpio/gpio-amzncpld.c
+++ b/drivers/gpio/gpio-amzncpld.c
@@ -728,7 +728,6 @@
 		girq->chip = &amzncpld_irq_chip;
 		girq->handler = handle_simple_irq;
 		girq->default_type = IRQ_TYPE_NONE;
-		girq->threaded = true;
 	}
 
 	ret = devm_gpiochip_add_data(cpld->dev, &cpld->gpio_chip, cpld);
